 :::
 
+### Resuming a shuffled epoch
+
+A job that is interrupted in the middle of an epoch and restarted would normally
+get a new order from {class}`~torch.utils.data.RandomSampler`, repeating some
+samples and missing others. To resume the interrupted epoch instead, save the
+sampler's {meth}`~torch.utils.data.RandomSampler.state_dict` with the rest of the
+checkpoint and pass it to
+{meth}`~torch.utils.data.RandomSampler.load_state_dict` of the new sampler before
+iterating. The next iteration then yields exactly the indices the interrupted one
+had not yielded yet, in the same order; later epochs are shuffled as usual. This
+works with and without replacement, and with or without a {attr}`generator`.
+
+```python
+loader = DataLoader(dataset, batch_size=32, shuffle=True)
+if resuming:
+    loader.sampler.load_state_dict(checkpoint["sampler"])
+for batch in loader:
+    train_step(batch)
+    if time_to_checkpoint:
+        checkpoint["sampler"] = loader.sampler.state_dict()
+        ...  # save the model, optimizer and checkpoint
+```
+
+The saved position counts the indices the sampler has yielded. With
+`num_workers=0` that is exactly what the training loop has received. With
+`num_workers > 0`, the loader draws indices ahead of the training loop to
+prefetch batches (up to `prefetch_factor * num_workers` batches), so the saved
+position is ahead of the model by those batches, and the resumed job will not
+see them.
+
 (loading-batched-and-non-batched-data)=
